<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amadeus VIII, Duke of Savoy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1383–1451</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Antipope Felix V · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amadeus VIII was count of Savoy from 1391 and was raised to duke by Emperor Sigismund in 1416. In 1418 he inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, uniting it with Savoy, and he issued a law code for his lands. He later retired to a hermitage and was elected antipope Felix V by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-basel">Council of Basel</a> in 1439, resigning in 1449.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amadeus-viii-duke-of-savoy.jpg" alt="Amadeus VIII, Duke of Savoy" width="320" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amadeus_VIII_of_Savoy_from_a_French_edition_of_Albertanus_of_Brescia%27s_De_doctrina_dicendi_et_tacendi_and_De_amore_et_dilectione_Dei_et_proximi_et_aliarum_rerum_et_de_forma_vitae.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amadeus was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chambery">Chambéry</a> on 4 September 1383, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadeus-vii-count-of-savoy">Amadeus VII, Count of Savoy</a>, and Bonne of Berry. He became count in 1391, when his father died on 2 November, a death attributed to poisoning or at least bad treatment by his physicians. His father's testament had named his own mother Bonne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> as regent rather than his wife, which set off a struggle at court between grandmother and mother that drew in the dukes of Berry, Bourbon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-of-france">Charles VI of France</a>. The grandmother acted as regent until 1397.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His early rule saw the centralisation of power and the territorial expansion of Savoy. In 1416 Sigismund, King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, raised him to Duke of Savoy, the first to hold the ducal title. In 1418 his cousin and brother-in-law Louis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, the last male of the elder branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>, died, leaving Amadeus as his heir-general and uniting the male lines of the house. From 1401 to 1422 he campaigned to recover the area around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> and Annecy, and he encouraged attempts to negotiate an end to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>.</p><p>After his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-of-burgundy">Mary of Burgundy</a> died in 1428, he founded the Order of Saint Maurice with six other knights in 1434. They lived in a quasi-monastic state at the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chateau-de-ripaille">Ripaille</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, under a rule he drew up himself, while his son Louis acted as regent of the duchy. Amadeus was sympathetic to conciliarism, the movement to have the Church governed by councils, and had close relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-basel">Council of Basel</a>. On 25 June 1439 the council deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-eugene-iv">Pope Eugene IV</a>, and on 5 November it elected Amadeus pope, with 26 votes in the ballot. He accepted on 5 February 1440 and took the name Felix V, naming his son Louis Duke of Savoy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Felix V left Basel for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a> in 1442, and the council later moved its sessions to Lausanne. After Eugene IV died in 1447, both sides favoured a settlement. On 7 April 1449 Felix V accepted the authority of Pope Nicholas V and resigned; he was named a cardinal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabina">Sabina</a> and papal legate in the territories of Savoy. He is the last historical antipope recognised by a significant part of the Catholic clergy. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> on 7 January 1451 and was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chateau-de-ripaille">Ripaille</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amadeus VIII of Savoy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Amadeus VIII</b>, the first Duke of Savoy, retired to a lakeside priory and was then elected antipope as Felix V by the Council of Basel in 1439.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1416 Emperor Sigismund raised <b>Amadeus VIII</b> to the rank of duke, and the county became the Duchy of Savoy.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-savoy">County of Savoy</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis died without a legitimate heir in 1418, and his lands returned to his cousin <b>Amadeus VIII</b> of the Duchy of Savoy, uniting Piedmont with Savoy.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-piedmont">Lordship of Piedmont</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chambery">Chambéry</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.57,5.92&amp;year=1427" aria-label="Chambéry on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4690 557 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Amadeus VIII, Duke of Savoy"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5140 827h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5140 827h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.57,5.92&amp;year=1427">What was here in 1427? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
